            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Converts a recording from one sample rate to another - 60 frames a second to 100, 120 to 30 - so that it describes the same movement over the same time with a different number of samples.\n\nIt does this properly: it filters out anything too fast for the new rate before thinning a recording out (so nothing turns into false slow wobbles), and draws a smooth curve between samples when adding more. Taking every other sample, or np.interp's straight lines, can do neither. Use it to bring recordings from different systems to a common rate, to match motion to an audio or video frame rate, or to thin a long take before analysing it.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.resample_poly <from rate> <to rate>\nDefaults: 60 to 100.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.resample_poly 120 60\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe recording (a list or array). Every input is resampled and sent.\n\nfrom rate (float box, also an inlet):\nThe recording's sample rate.\n\nto rate (float box, also an inlet):\nThe rate wanted. Only the ratio of the two matters: 60 to 100 is the same as 3 to 5.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way time runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column - for a buffer of frames x joints, use 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresampled:\nThe recording at the new rate: n samples become n x to / from.\n\nTHE ENDS:\nThe first and last few samples are slightly less exact, as with any filter.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nOne cycle of a wave in 12 samples (left), resampled from 60 to 100 a second: the same cycle in 20 samples (right), the curve unchanged. A new copy is sent every second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.interp resamples with straight lines; scipy.cubic_spline with a smooth curve but no filtering. subsample thins a live stream.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Converts a recording from one sample rate to another - 60 frames a second to 100, 120 to 30 - so that it describes the same movement over the same time with a different number of samples.\n\nIt does this properly: it filters out anything too fast for the new rate before thinning a recording out (so nothing turns into false slow wobbles), and draws a smooth curve between samples when adding more. Taking every other sample, or np.interp's straight lines, can do neither. Use it to bring recordings from different systems to a common rate, to match motion to an audio or video frame rate, or to thin a long take before analysing it.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.resample_poly <from rate> <to rate>\nDefaults: 60 to 100.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.resample_poly 120 60\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe recording (a list or array). Every input is resampled and sent.\n\nfrom rate (float box, also an inlet):\nThe recording's sample rate.\n\nto rate (float box, also an inlet):\nThe rate wanted. Only the ratio of the two matters: 60 to 100 is the same as 3 to 5.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way time runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column - for a buffer of frames x joints, use 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresampled:\nThe recording at the new rate: n samples become n x to / from.\n\nTHE ENDS:\nThe first and last few samples are slightly less exact, as with any filter.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nOne cycle of a wave in 12 samples (left), resampled from 60 to 100 a second: the same cycle in 20 samples (right), the curve unchanged. A new copy is sent every second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.interp resamples with straight lines; scipy.cubic_spline with a smooth curve but no filtering. subsample thins a live stream."
        },
